# A getter stands in front of `next`, so the literal is born without it and `next` is laid by a store behind the birth: `NewObject . PropDefine key="next" . FinishObject`. The text of the opening method still
# names one function for `next`: the one store under that name into that literal. It used to be read off the operands of the birth alone: nothing was expected, `next` was called by value each step and its
# answer was made and read by name: 4,222 instructions for 32 steps against 2,036.
# STATISTIC: NextMethodsTestedForFunctionWritten = 1
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: [[next]]: {{*}} = PropGet {{*}}key="next"
# CHECK: [[test]]: boolean @Boolean = TypeTest [[next]] testedType=f
# CHECK: isLoopHead=true
# CHECK: Branch [[test]] ->
# CHECK: = Narrowed [[next]], [[test]]
# CHECK: = GetCapture
# RUN(NEVER): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# NEVER-NOT: = NewObject
